<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · Anthem · c. 1755 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Yankee Doodle</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song British soldiers sang to make fun of American colonists, which the Americans took up as their own proud song.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional (earliest words attributed to Richard Shuckburgh; a 15-verse ballad of 1775 or 1776 attributed to Edward Bangs; many verses added by others)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the tune is older than the words; it may come from the Irish tune &quot;All the way to Galway&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>British soldiers in North America, then American colonists and revolutionaries</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Fort Crailo, Rensselaer, New York, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1755 or 1758 (the date of origin is disputed)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; the earliest words attributed to Richard Shuckburgh (c. 1755 or 1758))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The tune of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a>&quot; is thought to be much older than its words and was known across western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its second strain is identical to the Irish tune &quot;All the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a>&quot;, from which it may have come. There are rumours, unproven, that its earliest words came from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> harvest song of nonsense words in Dutch and English, perhaps as old as the 15th century. The word &quot;doodle&quot; appeared in English in the early 17th century and is thought to come from Low German words for playing music badly or for a fool. In the 1770s a &quot;macaroni&quot; was a man who dressed and spoke in an extravagantly fashionable way, a figure much mocked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> press.</p><p>The song is said to have been first sung by British officers to mock the colonial &quot;Yankees&quot; who served beside them in the French and Indian War. It was written at Fort Crailo, in Rensselaer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, around 1755 or 1758, by the British Army surgeon Richard Shuckburgh; one account says he wrote it after seeing the colonial troops of Colonel Thomas Fitch, son of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. The British sang it to cast the colonists as simpletons who thought a feather in the hat made them fashionable. Its sheet music asked for the words to be sung through the nose in a West Country drawl. The tune also appeared in 1762 in The Disappointment, a comic ballad opera from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>.</p><p>In the Revolution the song changed sides. One verse is tied to Thomas Ditson of Billerica, Massachusetts, whom soldiers of the 47th Regiment of Foot tarred and feathered in March 1775 for trying to buy a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>; Ditson later fought at Lexington and Concord, and Billerica calls itself the home of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a>&quot;. After Lexington and Concord a Boston newspaper reported that the pursuing Minutemen had made the British &quot;dance it till we were tired&quot;. Pro-British words were set to it after the Battle of Bunker Hill in June 1775, while American troops added verses mocking the British and praising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>. The Online Etymology Dictionary dates the current version to 1775 or 1776 and a 15-verse ballad by Edward Bangs, a Harvard student and Minuteman. The song was played at the British surrender at Saratoga in 1777, and by 1781 it had become a song of American national pride.</p><p>The song passed into the nursery: a variant was printed in the 1810 edition of Gammer Gurton's Garland, collected by Francis Douce. Before 3 March 1931, when &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a>&quot; became the official national anthem, it served with &quot;Hail, Columbia&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a>&quot; as one of the country's unofficial anthems. It is the state song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, and a bill introduced in the House of Representatives on 25 July 1999 recognised Billerica as &quot;America's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yankee-doodle">Yankee Doodle</a> Town&quot;. The Voice of America uses its tune as an interval signal; in his 1990 memoir Being Red, Howard Fast claimed that he chose it, &quot;as a joke&quot;, in 1943.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A light, mocking song about a country fellow who goes to town and thinks a feather in his cap makes him a gentleman. In its first use it laughed at the colonists' lack of polish; the same mockery, taken up by the colonists themselves, became a cheerful boast of plain American pride.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Father and I went down to camp, along with Captain Goodwin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And there we saw the men and boys as thick as hasty pudding.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yankee Doodle keep it up, Yankee Doodle dandy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Mind the music and the step, and with the girls be handy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And there was Captain Washington, upon a slapping stallion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A-giving orders to his men—I guess there was a million.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yankee Doodle, &amp;c.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And there they had a swamping gun, as big as a log of maple,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On a deuced little cart—a load for father's cattle.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yankee Doodle, &amp;c.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But I can't tell you half I see, they kept up such a smother:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So I took my hat off, made a bow, and scampered home to mother.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yankee Doodle, &amp;c.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Two_songs_entitled_Yankee_Doodle%2FYankee_Doodle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="jYGqiyEB8mQ" data-title="Yankee Doodle: Bobby Horton, sung as &quot;The Yankee's Return from Camp&quot;"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Bobby Horton, sung as &quot;The Yankee's Return from Camp&quot;</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Bobby Horton - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jYGqiyEB8mQ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Disappointment (ballad opera)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1762</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pro-British verses after the Battle of Bunker Hill</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1775</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ballad attributed to Edward Bangs</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1775 or 1776</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new verses (the current version)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Fall of Mexico (John H. Hewitt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1847</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Simple Simon; Jack and Jill; Lucy Locket (nursery rhymes)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">share the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Alps Ichiman Jaku (Japanese children's song)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">shares the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Voice of America interval signal</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Roger Ramjet (television cartoon theme)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Barney &amp; the Backyard Gang (theme)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Barney &amp; Friends (theme)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">E.T. (Toy-Box)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sampled in</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">All the way to Galway (Irish tune)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the melody may come from it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dutch harvest song</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">possibly 15th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">rumoured source of the earliest words (unproven)</span></li></ul></section>
